{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795700025,"nonce":"+RP0c7y+1X0EfQ4ZnmATSJLiQiw=","title":"The matched-DOF adaptivity test fails on main: its uniform reference cannot reach the adaptive DOF count","message":"tests/test_fem_adapt_quad.py::test_adaptivity_beats_uniform_refinement_at_matched_dofs fails on main (CPU, JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu) before it measures anything.\n\n**What goes wrong.** The test adapts an L-shape quad mesh (`remesh(theta=0.6, max_iters=4, refine_factor=1.6)`) and then searches a uniform sweep `size in np.linspace(0.045, 0.10, 12)` for the mesh whose vertex count is closest to the adaptive one. It then asserts the two are within 15 %. Observed: the adaptive mesh has 944 vertices, the finest uniform mesh in the sweep has 555, so `abs(555 - 944) = 389 \u003e 0.15 * 944` and the assertion \"the uniform comparison is not at matched DOFs\" fires. The comparison of the Ritz energies, which is the point of the test, never runs.\n\n**Reproduce.**\n`JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu pytest tests/test_fem_adapt_quad.py::test_adaptivity_beats_uniform_refinement_at_matched_dofs`\n\n**Expected.** The test either matches DOFs or fails on the error comparison. A guard that a sweep reached the target is right (it stops a test comparing unlike meshes), but the sweep has to be able to reach it. The adaptive run now produces more vertices than the sweep's finest uniform mesh (the sweep stops at size 0.045, about 555 vertices), so the adaptive loop and the sweep drifted apart, probably when the adaptive refinement or the vertex budget changed.\n\n**Where.** tests/test_fem_adapt_quad.py, the sweep at the `np.linspace(0.045, 0.10, 12)` line and the assertion after it. It is a test problem, not yet shown to be a library defect; whether adaptivity still beats uniform refinement at 944 vertices is unmeasured because the test exits first.\n\n**Workaround.** None needed for users. Locally, extend the sweep downward (size to about 0.03) to bring the uniform vertex count to 944.\n\n**Direction.** Derive the sweep from the target instead of fixing it: bracket the size by the adaptive vertex count (vertex count scales as 1/h^2), or bisect on size until the count is within the tolerance. Then run the energy comparison and report the numbers. If adaptivity no longer wins at that count, that is a separate regression to file. Keep the matched-DOF assertion so the sweep cannot silently become unmatched again.","files":null}]}